1a:["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-amber-200 text-gray-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Your task: "}],"Build the two-step habit that the official guidelines require and that prevents this class of error entirely."]}]
1b:["$","p","1",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"ICD-10-CM has two parts and both must be used. The Alphabetic Index is organized by condition and points you toward a code. The Tabular List is organized numerically and contains the actual code, along with the instructional notes that govern its use. The index never shows those notes, which is precisely why coding from it alone is unsafe."}]
1c:["$","p","2",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"The ICD-10-CM Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting are published annually alongside the code set and are the authority for how codes are selected and sequenced. They are not optional guidance — HIPAA requires their use for reporting, and payers apply them."}]
1d:["$","div","1",{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6","children":"Excludes1 and Excludes2"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"These two notes look almost identical and mean opposite things. Confusing them is one of the most consequential mistakes in diagnosis coding."}],["$","figure","1",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200","children":["$","table",null,{"className":"w-full text-left border-collapse","children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50","children":[["$","th","0",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Note"}],["$","th","1",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Means"}],["$","th","2",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Effect"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Excludes1"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"NOT CODED HERE — the two conditions cannot occur together"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Do not report both, unless the provider documents that the two conditions are unrelated to each other (Official Guidelines, Section I.A.12.a)"}]]}],["$","tr","1",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Excludes2"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Not included here — the condition is separate but may coexist"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Both codes may be reported together when both are documented"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"The two Excludes notes"}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"The way to remember it: Excludes1 means mutually exclusive — one or the other, not both. The guidelines allow one exception: when the two conditions are documented as unrelated to each other, both may be reported. Excludes2 means not part of this code, so if the patient has both conditions, code both. Payers and claim scrubbers often apply Excludes1 edits automatically, so a claim carrying an Excludes1 pair can be rejected without human review."}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-primary-200 bg-primary-50/50 p-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 mb-2","children":"Worked example"}],["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":"Deciding whether two codes may be reported together"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-5","children":"A patient is documented with two related conditions. The Tabular List entry for the first carries an Excludes2 note naming the second. May both be coded?"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":1}],"$L22"]}],"$L23","$L24","$L25"]}],"$L26","$L27"]}]]}]]}]
1e:["$","div","2",{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6","children":"Sequencing and the signs-and-symptoms rule"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"The first-listed diagnosis in the outpatient setting is the condition chiefly responsible for the services provided at that encounter. It is not necessarily the patient's most serious problem, and not necessarily the first one mentioned in the note."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"The signs-and-symptoms rule follows from that. When a definitive diagnosis is established, code the diagnosis and not the symptoms that led to it — the symptoms are integral to it. When no definitive diagnosis is reached, code the symptoms, because that is what is actually known."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"In the outpatient setting, conditions documented as 'probable,' 'suspected,' 'rule out,' or 'questionable' are not coded as though they existed. Code the signs, symptoms, or test findings that prompted the workup instead. This differs from inpatient rules, where uncertain diagnoses documented at discharge may be coded as if established — a genuine difference between settings that catches people who move from one to the other."]}],["$","$L28","3",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"A patient presents with chest pain. After evaluation the provider documents 'chest pain, rule out angina.' What should be coded?","options":["Angina, since that is the condition being investigated","Chest pain, because the angina is unconfirmed and this is an outpatient encounter","Both chest pain and angina, to capture the full picture","Neither, until the workup is complete"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In the outpatient setting, a 'rule out' condition is not coded as though it were established. Code the documented symptom — chest pain — because that is what is actually known at this encounter. Coding the angina would assert a diagnosis the record does not support.","optionRationales":["Coding an unconfirmed condition in the outpatient setting asserts something the documentation explicitly does not establish.","Correct. Code the symptom; the suspected condition is not coded until it is confirmed.","Adding the unconfirmed diagnosis has the same problem on its own, and the symptom would in any case be integral to it were it confirmed.","The encounter happened and services were provided, so it must be coded. The symptom is the correct code."]}}]]}]]}]
1f:["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-12","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[40px] sm:leading-[1.05]","children":"Challenge"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft","children":"Apply what you've learned in this lesson."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-md border border-gray-200 p-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Work with the actual guidelines. CMS publishes the ICD-10-CM Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting free each year."}],["$","ol","1",{"start":"$undefined","className":"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 marker:font-semibold marker:text-primary-600","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Find one Excludes1 note and one Excludes2 note in the Tabular List. Write out each, and explain in your own words what a coder may and may not do in each case."}],["$","li","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"For an encounter documented as 'fatigue and weight loss, rule out malignancy,' decide what to code and write two sentences justifying it with reference to the guidelines."}],["$","li","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"A note documents both type 2 diabetes and diabetic neuropathy. Look up how the code set expects that relationship to be reported, and explain what a combination code is."}],["$","li","3",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Describe the two-step lookup process in your own words, and state one specific thing you would miss by skipping the Tabular List."}]]}]]}]}]]}]
